So far, you've been using the PowerShell Console to call one command at a time. (Page 37)

For bigger projects, having to call each command individually is too time-consuming. 
Luckily, you can combine commands so you can call them as a single unit. You can use the PowerShell pipeline or saving your code in external scripts. 

Starting a Windows Service. 

  To illustrate why you'd want to combine commands, you'll start by doing a simple example the old way. You'll use two commands: Get-Service, which queries Windows 
  services and returns information about them; and Start-Service, which starts Windows services. 

  $serviceName = "wuauserv'
  Get-Service -Name $serviceName
  Start-Service -Name $serviceName

  You can run Get-Service just to make sure PowerShell doesn't throw any errors. Chances are the service is already running. 
  If it is, Start-service will simply return control to the console. 

  You can imagine how monotonous it might get if you were working with hundreds od services. 

Using the Pipeline

  The first way to simplify your code is by chaining together commands by using the PowerShell pipeline, a tool that allows you to send the output of one command
  directly to antoher command as input. To use the pipeline, use the pipe operator (|) between two commands like so:

  command1 | command2

  Here is the output of command1 is piped into command2, becoming command2's input. The final command in the pipeline will output to the console. (Page 38)
  Many shell scripting languages including cmd.exe and bash, use a pipeline. But what makes the pipeline in Powershell unique is that it passes objects and not simple    strings. 

Piping Objects between Commands

  To send the output of Get-Service into Start-Service: 

  Get-Service -Name 'wuauserv' | Start-Service

  In this example, you don't have to specify any parameters because PowerShell takes care of that for you. It looks at the output of Get-Service, decides what values   it should pass to Start-Service, and matches the value to the parameters that Start-Service takes. 

  You can could also run it without any parameters. 

  'wuauserv' | Get-Service | Start-Service

  Powershell sends the string wuauserv into Get-Service and the output of Get-Service into Start-Service - all without you having to specify a thing!
  You've combined three separate commands into a single line, but you'll still have to reenter that line for every service you want to start. 

Piping Arrays Between Commands

  If you create a text file in notepad called services.txt that contain the strings Wuauserve and W32Time on separates lines you have created a file with services you
  can run. 
  A file like this can contain as many services as you like. To disaply the file to your powershell window, use the Get-Content cmdlet's Path Parameter. (Page 39).

  Get-Content -Path C:\Services.txt

  The Get-Content command reads in a file line by line, adding each line to an array and then returning that array. 
  You can also use the pipeline to pass the array that Get-Contents returns into the Get-Service command. 

  Get-Content -Path C:\Services.txt | Get-Service

  The Get-Content command is reading in the text file and spitting out an array. 
  But instead of sending the array itself througgh the pipeline, PowerShell unwraps it, sending each item in the array individually through the pipeline. 

  This allows you to execute the same commands for every item in the array. By putting every service you want to start in your text file, and tacking on an extra
  | Start-Service you have a insgle comand that can start as many services as you need. 

  Get-Content -Path C:\Services.txt | Get-Service | Start-Service

  There's no limit to how many commands you can stitch together using the pipeline. But if you find yourself going over five, you may need to rethink your approach. 
  Note that although the pipeline is powefule, it won't work everywhere: most PowerShell commands accept only certain types of pipeline input, and some don't 
  accept any at all.

Looking at Parameter Binding 

  When you pass parameters to a command, PowerShell initiates a process known as parameter binding, in which it matches each object you pass into the command to the 
  various parameters specified by the command's creator. 

  For a Powershell command to accept pipeline input the person who writes the command -whether that's Microsoft or you has to explicitly build in pipeline support
  for one or more parameters. 
  You'll notice an error if you attempt to pipe information into a command that doesn't have pipeline support on any parameter, or if Powershell cannot find a 
  suitable binding. 

  'string' | Get-Process

  You should see that the command won't accept pipeline input. 
  To see if using the pipeline is even possible, you can look at a command's full help content by using the Full paramter on the Get-Help command. 

  Get-Help -Name Get-Service -Full

  You should get quite a bit of output. Scroll down to the PARAMETERS section. This section lists information about each parameter and gives you more information
  than you'd get by not using the Detailed or Full parameter at all. 

  There's a lot of information here, but the focus is on the Accept pipeline input. 
  This field tells you whether a parameter accepts a pipeline input or not; if the parameter didn't accept pipeline input, you'd see false next to this field.
  There can be even more information such as if the parameter accepts pipeline input both via ByValue and ByPropertyName. 

  -Name <string[]>

  -ComputerName <string[]>

  ComputerName only accepts ByPropertyName. (Page 41).

  The computer name parameter allows you to specify which computer you'd like to run Get-Service on. 
  This parameter also accepts a string type. 

  So how does Powershell know that you mean the service name and no the computer computer name when you do something like the following:

  'wuauserv' | Get-Service

   Powershell matches pipeline input to parameters in two ways. 
   The first is ByValue, which means that PowerShell will look at the type of object passed in and interpret it accordingly.
   Because Get-Service specifies that it accepts the Name paramter via ByValue, it will interpet any string passed to it as Name unless otherwise specified. 
   Because parameters passed via ByValue depend on the type of input, each paramter passed ByValue can be on type only. 

    ByValue = Match by Type
    ByPropertyName = Match by PropertyName
      (Powershell does this in the background) Page 41. 

    The second way PowerShell will match a parameter from the pipeline is via by PropertyName. 
    In this case, Powershell will look at the object passed in, and if it has a property with the appropriate name (ComputerName, in this case), then it will look at     the value for that property and accept that value as the parameter. 

    So if you wanted to pass in both a service name and a computer name to Get-Service, you could createa a PSCustomerObject and pass it in. 

$serviceObject = [PSCustomObject]@{name = 'wuauserv' ; ComputerName = 'SERV1'}
$serviceObject | Get-Service

"If the labels match, pass the values" 

    By looking at a command's parameter specifications, and using a hashtable to cleanly store the ones you need, you'll be able to use the pipeline string together all sorts of commands. 

Writing Scripts 

    Scripts are external files that store a sequence of commands, which you can run by entering a single line in the PowerShell Console. 

PS> C:\FolderPathToScript\script.ps1

    Although there's nothing you can do in a script that you can't do in the console, it's much easier to run a single command using a script than it is to type several thousand commands. 
    Not to mention that if you want to change anything in your code, or you make a mistake you'll need to retype those commands again. 
    But before you start writing scripts, you have to change some PowerShell settings to make sure you can them. Page 42. 

Setting the Execution Policy 

    By default, PowerShell does not allow you to run any scripts. 
    If you try to run an external script in the default PowerShell installation, you'll encounter an error. 

    This frustating error message is the result of PowerShell's execution policy, a security measure that decides which scripts can run. 
    The execution policy has four main configurations: 

  Restricted: This configuration policy, which is the default, doesn't allow you to run scripts. 
  AllSigned: This configuration allows you to run only scripts that have been cryptographically signed by a trusted party.
  RemoteSigned: This configuration allows you to run any script you write, and any script you download as long as it's been cryptographically sgined by a trusted party
  Unrestricted: This configuration allows you to run any script. 

    To see which execution policy your machine is currently using, run the command: 

Get-ExecutionPolicy

    Chances are you get restricted when you run this command. 
    For our purposes, the execution policy should be RemoteSigned. 
    This will allow you to run any scripts you write, while also ensuring that you use only external scripts that come from trusted sources.
    To change the exeuction policy, you use the Set-ExecutionPolicy command and pass in the policy you want. 
    Note that you'll need to be running this command as an administrator. 
    You'll need to perform this command only once, as the setting is saved in the registry.
    If you're in a large active directory environment, the execution policy can also be set across many computers at once by using Group Policy. 

Set-ExecutionPolicy -ExecutionPolicy RemoteSigned

    Run the Get-ExeuctionPolicy command again in order to verify that you successfully changed the policy to RemoteSigned. 
    The policy should stay at RemoteSigned until you want to change it again. 

Script Signing

    A script signature is an encrypted string appended to the end of the script as a comment; these signatures are generated by a certificate installed on your 
    computer. 
    When you set your policy to AllSigned or RemoteSigned, you will be able to run only those scripts that are properly signed. 
    Signing a source lets PowerShell know that the script's source is reliable and that the author of the script is who they say they are. 


    You should sign any script that you're creating and executing in a professional environment. 

Scripting in PowerShell

    Now that your execution policy is set, it's time to write a script and execute it in the console. 
    You can write PowerShell scripts in any text editor you like, but the most convenient way to write PowerShell scripts is by usingthe PowerShell Integrated Scripting environment. 
    Technically, the ISE is deprecated, but it comes preinstalled with Windows. 

  Using the PowerShell ISE

    The command to start PowerShell ISE is 

powershell_ise.exe (Page 44)

    An interactive console should open up. 
    To add a script, click File > New. 
    The screen should split, with a white panel opening above the console. 

    Click File > Save and save the new file. 
    Notice that you save your script with the extension .ps1; this extension tells your system that the file is a PowerShell script. 

    You'll be entering all the text for your script in the white panel. 
    The PowerShell ISE allows you to edit and run the scriptin the same window, which can save you a lot of annoying back and forth as you're editing. 
    The PowerShell ISE has many more features. 

    PowerShell scripts are simple text files. 
    It doesn't matter which which text editor is used, so long as you use the correct PowerShell syntax. 

Writing Your First Script

    Write-Host 'Hello, I am in a script'

    Notice there's no PS> at the start of the line. 
    From here on out, that's how you'll know whether we're working in the console or writing a script.
